===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The state of being [[secluded]] from the presence, sight, or knowledge of others.
#: {{ux|en|I need my '''privacy''', so please stay out of my room.}}
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=II|passage=Carried somehow, somewhither, for some reason, on these surging floods, were these travelers,{{nb...}}. Even such a boat as the ''Mount Vernon'' offered a total deck space so cramped as to leave secrecy or '''privacy''' well out of the question, even had the motley and democratic assemblage of passengers been disposed to accord either.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=November and December|title="Duplex Roomette" Sleeping Cars|journal=Railway Magazine|page=324|text=It is realised that the old Pullman standard sleeper, with its convertible "sections", each containing upper and lower berths, and with no greater '''privacy''' at night than the curtains drawn along both sides of a middle aisle, has had its day.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Freedom from unwanted or undue disturbance of one's private life.
#: {{ux|en|Those prying neighbours have robbed me of my sense of '''privacy'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Freedom from damaging publicity, public scrutiny, surveillance, and disclosure of personal information, usually by a government or a private organization.
#: {{ux|en|'''Privacy''' is assumed by many to be among common-law rights.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=May 6 1998|author={{w|Douglas Watkinson}}; et al|chapter={{w|Death in Disguise}}|title={{w|Midsomer Murders}}
|passage=''Trixie Channing:'' Good God. Is there no '''privacy'''? <br> ''DCI Tom Barnaby:'' No, not in a murder case.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|obsolete}} A place of [[seclusion]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|legal}} A relationship between parties seen as being a result of their mutual interest or participation in a given transaction, contract etc.
#: {{syn|en|privity}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[secrecy|Secrecy]].
# {{lb|en|countable|obsolete}} A private matter.
#: {{syn|en|secret}}
#* {{RQ:Ovid Epistles|chapter=Preface|page=7|passage=It ſeems more probable that ''Ovid'' vvas either the Confident of ſome other paſſion, or that he had ſtumbled by ſome inadvertency, upon the '''privacies''' of ''Livia'', and ſeen her in a Bath: {{...}}}}

